---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
---

<BaseLayout
  title="About QuiltClarity — Transparent Quilt Fabric Math"
  description="Learn what QuiltClarity calculates, how it protects project privacy, and why every important quilting assumption remains visible."
>
  <article class="page article-page">
    <header class="hero compact-hero">
      <p class="eyebrow">About QuiltClarity</p>
      <h1>Quilting calculations you can inspect</h1>
      <p class="lede">
        QuiltClarity is a free, static-first set of tools for fabric yardage,
        rectangular cutting plans, and common quilt-construction calculations.
      </p>
    </header>
    <div class="article-body">
      <section>
        <h2>What the site does</h2>
        <p>
          The fabric cutting planner turns piece requirements into a shopping
          quantity, cutting list, and diagram from one deterministic layout.
          Seven focused calculators handle repeated-piece yardage, backing,
          binding, HSTs, block count, borders, and sashing.
        </p>
        <p>
          Important defaults—such as usable width, seam allowance, rotation,
          safety allowance, backing overage, and purchase increment—are visible
          and editable. Results explain the assumptions used.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>What the site does not claim</h2>
        <p>
          The cutting planner uses a bounded practical heuristic. It prioritizes
          valid, strip-friendly cuts and deterministic results; it does not
          claim to prove the global minimum fabric use. Calculator defaults are
          planning conventions, not substitutes for pattern instructions,
          measuring the actual quilt top, or a longarmer's requirements.
        </p>
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>Privacy and operation</h2>
        <p>
          Calculations run in the browser. Projects may be saved in this
          device's local storage, but QuiltClarity has no account, upload,
          application backend, or cloud project store. Analytics, when a
          deployment connects a provider, use fixed tool/action identifiers and
          exclude project names, labels, notes, measurements, quantities, and
          results.
        </p>
        {
          import.meta.env.PUBLIC_SIMPLE_ANALYTICS_ENABLED === 'true' &&
            import.meta.env.PUBLIC_ROBOTS_NOINDEX !== 'true' && (
              <p>
                Production measurement uses Simple Analytics to count page
                visits and fixed tool actions. We send the page's canonical path
                and allowed categories, without project content, URL queries,
                shared-project fragments, cookies, or visitor IDs. The provider
                receives normal network metadata such as IP addresses and HTTP
                browser headers. Do Not Track and Global Privacy Control disable
                this measurement. See the provider's{' '}
                <a href="https://docs.simpleanalytics.com/data-collection">
                  data collection documentation
                </a>
                .
              </p>
            )
        }
      </section>
      <section>
        <h2>How to verify or challenge a result</h2>
        <p>
          Read the <a href="/methodology/">calculation methodology</a>,
          reproduce the worked examples, and check the visible assumptions in
          your result. The <a href="/corrections/">feedback page</a> has the current
          status of feedback submissions.
        </p>
      </section>
    </div>
  </article>
</BaseLayout>
